{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8725b3577adc361","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013 (FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. Triage (Step 0) evaluated and\n13\tnot triggered: the strategy spans two components (KServe, Platform Operator) and\n14\tmultiple architecturally distinct work streams (image remediation, CI enforcement,\n15\tcompliance evidence process, documentation), with mandatory external process gates.\n16\t\n17\t## Decomposition Notes\n18\t\n19\t- **No Investigation epics.** The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None on\n20\t  scope\". The image-level crypto audit (which images link non-validated crypto) is\n21\t  discovery work whose outcome changes the volume of remediation, not which epics\n22\t  exist or what they do — per the Step 3 decision rule it is folded into the\n23\t  remediation epics as scope and acceptance criteria, and its work product (the\n24\t  module inventory) is a required input to the evidence package.\n25\t- **No onboarding chain.** KServe is already Konflux-onboarded (corroborated by\n26\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`). Platform\n27\t  Operator has no architecture-context file, but it is in the canonical RHAI\n28\t  component list and the strategy premise is that it ships images today (\"...link\n29\t  non-validated crypto libraries in several images\") — it is treated as an existing\n30\t  in-platform component, not a new one. See Health Warnings.\n31\t- **Component mappings.** All component values are verbatim from\n32\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`. The evidence-package epic spans the whole serving\n33\t  stack; it is assigned the single most-relevant canonical name `KServe` (the\n34\t  serving stack's primary component). The documentation epic is assigned the\n35\t  canonical `Documentation` component.\n36\t- **Priority derivation.** E006 (docs-authoring) derives P0 from the strategy\n37\t  frontmatter priority Critical per the docs-authoring priority exception; it does\n38\t  not push priority upstream (Rule 11 edges do not trigger inheritance), so the\n39\t  P1 CI epics remain P1.\n40\t- **Team assignment.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table. KServe epics are\n41\t  assigned to RHOAI Serving (owning team per the KServe architecture-context file).\n42\t  Platform Operator epics are assigned to RHOAI Platform — an assumption flagged in\n43\t  Health Warnings.\n44\t\n45\t## Epic List\n46\t\n47\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n48\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n50\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of the Platform Operator image | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS regression gating check for KServe serving-stack builds | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS regression gating check for Platform Operator builds | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P1 |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | FIPS support posture documentation for the model serving stack | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n55\t\n56\t## Dependency DAG\n57\t\n58\t```mermaid\n59\tgraph TD\n60\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 KServe FIPS remediation] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 Evidence package]\n61\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 Platform Operator FIPS remediation] --> E005\n62\t    E001 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 Documentation]\n63\t    E002 --> E006\n64\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 KServe CI gating] --> E006\n65\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 Platform Operator CI gating] --> E006\n66\t    E005 --> E006\n67\t```\n68\t\n69\tCritical path: E001/E002 → E005 → E006 (3 epics).\n70\t\n71\t## DAG Justification\n72\t\n73\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n74\t|------|------|-----------|\n75\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The evidence package is assembled from the KServe remediation's primary deliverables — the module inventory and the FIPS verification test results. It cannot be assembled before they exist. |\n76\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same for the Platform Operator remediation's inventory and verification results. |\n77\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; docs-authoring is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n78\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n79\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n80\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n81\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n82\t\n83\tNon-edges (deliberate):\n84\t\n85\t- **No remediation → CI-gating edges (Rules 14/15).** The CI gating epics implement\n86\t  a detection check against the validated-module criteria; they are independent of\n87\t  when remediation lands and are most useful while it is in flight. They run in\n88\t  parallel in separate repos/areas.\n89\t- **No edge for build attestations (Rule 13 exception).** The signed build\n90\t  attestations consumed by the evidence package are build-pipeline outputs consumed\n91\t  as content — the coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E005 (\"the\n92\t  evidence package includes the signed build attestations produced by the\n93\t  serving-stack build pipelines\"), not as a DAG edge.\n94\t- **No konflux-onboarding / license-validation / repo-onboarding epics.** KServe is\n95\t  already Konflux-onboarded and Platform Operator is an existing shipping component;\n96\t  no onboarding chain applies (Rule 20 not triggered).\n97\t\n98\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n99\t\n100\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) |\n101\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|\n102\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All serving-stack images run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules (no non-validated crypto in the request path). | RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 (KServe portion), RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 (Platform Operator portion) |\n103\t| HLR-2 | P0 | A compliance evidence package (module inventory, build attestations, test results) is assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab. | RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 |\n104\t| HLR-3 | P1 | CI enforces FIPS regressions: a gating check fails any serving-stack build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage. | RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 (KServe builds), RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 (Platform Operator builds) |\n105\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation states the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites. | RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 |\n106\t\n107\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-1 and HLR-3 each map to\n108\ttwo epics because the work spans two components owned by different teams (epic\n109\tboundary rule 1); each portion is independently schedulable in its own repo.\n110\t\n111\t## Health Warnings\n112\t\n113\t- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n114\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional\n115\t  Requirements, and no Technical Approach; the Affected Components section is a\n116\t  bare list without per-component change/owner detail. The decomposition proceeds\n117\t  from the HLRs and problem statement; epic acceptance criteria were derived from\n118\t  the HLR text.\n119\t- **Team assumption (verify before scheduling).** The Platform Operator epics\n120\t  (RHAISTRAT-9013-E002, RHAISTRAT-9013-E004) are assigned to \"RHOAI Platform\" by\n121\t  assumption — the strategy names no owning team for Platform Operator.\n122\t- **Scope wording — \"serving data plane\".** The problem statement says \"KServe\n123\t  components and the serving data plane link non-validated crypto libraries\", but\n124\t  the Affected Components section lists only KServe and Platform Operator. The\n125\t  serving data plane is treated as within the KServe component scope. If Serving\n126\t  Runtimes images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit — KServe dependencies per the\n127\t  architecture context) are intended to be in scope, additional remediation and CI\n128\t  epics are needed and this decomposition must be revisited.\n129\t- **Architecture-context coverage gap.** Only\n130\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` is provisioned;\n131\t  no context file exists for Platform Operator or Documentation. Platform Operator\n132\t  is treated as an existing in-platform component (canonical component list member\n133\t  shipping images today per the strategy premise) rather than a new component\n134\t  needing an onboarding chain. AI-signal \"architecture claims\" is scored 0 for the\n135\t  Platform Operator and Documentation epics (neither corroborated nor contradicted).\n136\t- **Planning note (epic size).** The KServe remediation epic\n137\t  (RHAISTRAT-9013-E001) could run above the ~2-week epic-size guideline depending\n138\t  on how many images and request-path call sites the audit finds (\"several images\"\n139\t  per the strategy, no count given). Kept as one logical change: a split would\n140\t  duplicate the audit pass and fragment a single mechanical conversion across the\n141\t  component. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the audit finds materially\n142\t  more affected images or request-path call sites than a single sprint absorbs.\n143\t- **External schedule risk (not an acceptance condition).** Certification lab\n144\t  turnaround is historically six to nine months and security-review-board cadence\n145\t  is externally controlled; both are recorded as risks on\n146\t  RHAISTRAT-9013-E005, whose acceptance criteria are written at the\n147\t  engineering-controlled endpoint (assembly and confirmed submission), per the\n148\t  externally-controlled-precondition rule.\n149\t\n150\t## Tiered Delivery\n151\t\n152\tNot applicable. The external accredited laboratory gates the *certification\n153\toutcome*, which is outside the HLR scope — HLR-2's endpoint is assembly and\n154\tsubmission of the evidence package. All in-strategy work (HLR-1 through HLR-4) is\n155\tdeliverable without the lab's response, so there is no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split and\n156\tno upstream epic with uncertain timing gating downstream engineering work.\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa6422f5086bb052","agent_id":"a090fa2fed4ccaea4","session_id":"507c58fb-94b1-4432-a69d-7f745e4ce707","uuid":"a62cdf0e-2a6c-4b12-9345-07ca3950c2f7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:11:31.154Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8725b3577adc361","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
