ncy.\n67\t\n68\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n69\tComponent/team tuples are clean: KServe/RHOAI Serving (E001, E003, E005),\n70\tPlatform Operator/RHOAI Platform (E002, E004), Documentation/RHOAI Serving\n71\t(E006). The per-component split of HLR-1 and HLR-3 work follows boundary rule 1\n72\tand keeps each portion independently schedulable. E005 consolidates\n73\tcross-component inventories into one evidence package — one logical change (a\n74\tsingle submission to one lab), correctly kept whole and documented in the\n75\tDecomposition Notes. No scope duplication between siblings: E001/E002 produce\n76\tper-component inventories that E005 consumes (producer/consumer, not\n77\tduplication); E003/E004 gate different pipelines. No work-product type mixing —\n78\tdocs authoring is isolated in E006. The one above-guideline epic (E001) is kept\n79\tas a single logical change WITH a concrete recorded re-split trigger (\"re-split\n80\tif the audit finds materially more affected images or request-path call sites\n81\tthan a single sprint absorbs\") — a managed trade-off, within guideline.\n82\t\n83\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n84\tNo Investigation epics — correct per the Step 3 decision rule: the strategy's\n85\tOpen Questions section is \"None on scope\", and the image-level crypto audit is\n86\tinformational discovery whose outcome changes remediation volume, not which\n87\tepics exist or what they do (rule 4: reclassified as scope/ACs on the\n88\tremediation epics, with the module inventory as a deliverable feeding E005).\n89\tEvery Implementation epic produces a concrete artifact (remediated images,\n90\tgating check, evidence package, published docs). No `gated_by` anywhere and no\n91\tbranch files, so gating metadata is trivially consistent. E006's\n92\t`implementation_type: docs-authoring` is correct. The externally controlled lab\n93\tand review-board cadence are correctly modeled as risks on E005, not as gates\n94\ton engineering work.\n95\t\n96\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n97\tAll six epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric);\n98\tno epic mixes signal sets. Frontmatter values match the ai-signals rationale\n99\tfiles exactly for all six epics. Spot-checks against the rubric: E005's\n100\t`existing_foundation: -1` (greenfield package — \"we have no compliance evidence\n101\tpackage\") and `external_dependency`/`human_process_gates: -1` (external lab,\n102\treview board) are well grounded in the strategy; E001/E003's\n103\t`architecture_claims: +1` correctly cite\n104\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` (GA maturity,\n105\t\"Konflux onboarded: yes\" — both verified present in the file); E002/E004/E006's\n106\t`architecture_claims: 0` is the correct score because no context file covers\n107\tPlatform Operator or Documentation (score 0 is valid only when no context file\n108\tcovers the components — satisfied), and the coverage gap is disclosed in the\n109\tsummary's Health Warnings rather than papered over with an invalid \"no claims\"\n110\trationale. `open_questions` values are calibrated (0 where remediation choices\n111\tremain but don't change the approach; +1 where HLR text fully specifies the\n112\tdeliverable). Computed totals/classifications were not checked, per the\n113\tpipeline-computes-them rule.\n114\t\n115\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n116\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text. Rule-mandated ACs: E006\n117\t(docs-authoring) has the \"technically reviewed against the implemented FIPS\n118\tposture\" AC (rule 24); no epic has `konflux-onboarding` in its dependency chain\n119\tso rule 25 is not triggered (E001/E002 nonetheless carry build-pipeline ACs);\n120\tthe strategy replaces no existing capability, so rule 23 rollback/feature-flag\n121\tACs do not apply. The canonical soft-AC trap is handled correctly: the\n122\texternally controlled lab turnaround and review-board cadence are recorded in\n123\tE005's Risks/Notes and the summary's Health Warnings, and E005's ACs are\n124\twritten at the engineering-controlled endpoint (assembly and confirmed\n125\tsubmission) — no AC states an externally granted precondition. No epic body\n126\treferences sibling epics by draft ID.\n127\t\n128\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n129\tAll strategy scope is covered: the four HLRs, the per-component split of HLR-1\n130\tand HLR-3, the out-of-scope boundaries (workbenches/pipelines/training, Common\n131\tCriteria) are carried into E006's scope, and the externally controlled schedule\n132\tnote from Open Questions is carried forward as risks on E005. HLR-4 requires\n133\tdocumentation and the docs-authoring epic exists (Rule 11 satisfied). Tiered\n134\tdelivery was evaluated and correctly rejected with reasoning: HLR-2's endpoint\n135\tis assembly and submission, so certification-lab timing gates nothing in\n136\tstrategy scope. One minor, already flagged by the decomposer in Health\n137\tWarnings: the problem statement's \"serving data plane\" wording versus an\n138\tAffected Components list of only KServe and Platform Operator — the\n139\tdecomposition treats the data plane as within KServe scope and flags that\n140\tServing Runtimes images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit, listed as KServe dependencies\n141\tin the architecture context) would require additional epics if intended in\n142\tscope. The ambiguity is disclosed, not silently dropped; the correction is to\n143\tconfirm scope with the strategy owner before scheduling, not to change the\n144\tdecomposition.\n145\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-895683de6985d69b","agent_id":"a0d70266d0e7a7c96","session_id":"507c58fb-94b1-4432-a69d-7f745e4ce707","uuid":"be006f8f-356d-454f-a21c-3717bff337c5","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:15:45.561Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b21ea8074276a58b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAIST