{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5a6b3fa47e5d8a6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013 (FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack)\n10\t\n11\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. Triage (Step 0) evaluated and\n12\tnot triggered: the strategy spans two components (KServe, Platform Operator) and\n13\tmultiple architecturally distinct work streams (image remediation, CI enforcement,\n14\tcompliance evidence process, documentation), with mandatory external process gates.\n15\t\n16\t## Decomposition Notes\n17\t\n18\t- **No Investigation epics.** The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None on\n19\t  scope\". The image-level crypto audit (which images link non-validated crypto) is\n20\t  discovery work whose outcome changes the volume of remediation, not which epics\n21\t  exist or what they do — per the Step 3 decision rule it is folded into the\n22\t  remediation epics as scope and acceptance criteria, and its work product (the\n23\t  module inventory) is a required input to the evidence package.\n24\t- **No onboarding chain.** KServe is already Konflux-onboarded (corroborated by\n25\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`). Platform\n26\t  Operator has no architecture-context file, but it is in the canonical RHAI\n27\t  component list and the strategy premise is that it ships images today (\"...link\n28\t  non-validated crypto libraries in several images\") — it is treated as an existing\n29\t  in-platform component, not a new one. See Health Warnings.\n30\t- **Component mappings.** All component values are verbatim from\n31\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`. The evidence-package epic spans the whole serving\n32\t  stack; it is assigned the single most-relevant canonical name `KServe` (the\n33\t  serving stack's primary component). The documentation epic is assigned the\n34\t  canonical `Documentation` component.\n35\t- **Priority derivation.** E006 (docs-authoring) derives P0 from the strategy\n36\t  frontmatter priority Critical per the docs-authoring priority exception; it does\n37\t  not push priority upstream (Rule 11 edges do not trigger inheritance), so the\n38\t  P1 CI epics remain P1.\n39\t- **Team assignment.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table. KServe epics are\n40\t  assigned to RHOAI Serving (owning team per the KServe architecture-context file).\n41\t  Platform Operator epics are assigned to RHOAI Platform — an assumption flagged in\n42\t  Health Warnings.\n43\t\n44\t## Epic List\n45\t\n46\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n47\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of the Platform Operator image | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n50\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS regression gating check for KServe serving-stack builds | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS regression gating check for Platform Operator builds | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P1 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | FIPS support posture documentation for the model serving stack | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n54\t\n55\t## Dependency DAG\n56\t\n57\t```mermaid\n58\tgraph TD\n59\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 KServe FIPS remediation] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 Evidence package]\n60\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 Platform Operator FIPS remediation] --> E005\n61\t    E001 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 Documentation]\n62\t    E002 --> E006\n63\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 KServe CI gating] --> E006\n64\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 Platform Operator CI gating] --> E006\n65\t    E005 --> E006\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tCritical path: E001/E002 → E005 → E006 (3 epics).\n69\t\n70\t## DAG Justification\n71\t\n72\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n73\t|------|------|-----------|\n74\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The evidence package is assembled from the KServe remediation's primary deliverables — the module inventory and the FIPS verification test results. It cannot be assembled before they exist. |\n75\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same for the Platform Operator remediation's inventory and verification results. |\n76\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; docs-authoring is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n77\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n78\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n79\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n80\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same. |\n81\t\n82\tNon-edges (deliberate):\n83\t\n84\t- **No remediation → CI-gating edges (Rules 14/15).** The CI gating epics implement\n85\t  a detection check against the validated-module criteria; they are independent of\n86\t  when remediation lands and are most useful while it is in flight. They run in\n87\t  parallel in separate repos/areas.\n88\t- **No edge for build attestations (Rule 13 exception).** The signed build\n89\t  attestations consumed by the evidence package are build-pipeline outputs consumed\n90\t  as content — the coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E005 (\"the\n91\t  evidence package includes the signed build attestations produced by the\n92\t  serving-stack build pipelines\"), not as a DAG edge.\n93\t- **No konflux-onboarding / license-validation / repo-onboarding epics.** KServe is\n94\t  already Konflux-onboarded and Platform Operator is an existing shipping component;\n95\t  no onboarding chain applies (Rule 20 not