+1 on E006 with an explicit rationale for scoring the Documentation epic against the KServe context file its content documents. E005's unfavorable signals (existing_foundation -1 for the greenfield package per the problem statement, external_dependency -1 for the lab, human_process_gates -1 for review-board sign-off) are each grounded in strategy text. E003 vs E004 differentiation (change_specificity +1 vs 0, based on whether the pipeline surface is documented) is reasoned, not arbitrary. Score arithmetic and thresholds were not checked, per the pipeline-computes rule.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text. The externally-controlled-precondition rule is applied correctly: lab turnaround and review-board cadence are recorded as Risks/Notes on E005 and as a Health Warning, and E005's ACs are written at the engineering-controlled endpoint (assembly and confirmed submission) — no schedule constraint is misfiled as an AC. E006 has the rule-mandated technical-review AC. E001–E004 ACs are verifiable conditions on the epics' own deliverables (image scans, build success, functional verification, check polarities). No rollback/feature-flag AC is required — no epic replaces one component with an alternative implementation; the crypto remediation is a rebuild against validated modules with the existing pipeline as the verification gate. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (all cross-references use full IDs or artifact names).\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is covered: the four HLRs, the audit/remediation/verification work from the problem statement, the evidence-package submission route (security review board → external lab), and the out-of-scope boundaries (workbenches/pipelines/training, Common Criteria) which are explicitly restated in E006's scope. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped: the six-to-nine-month lab turnaround and externally-controlled review